WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.750
Both of these images are groundbreaking.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:03.750
And connected.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:09.940
On the left, you’ve got the world through
Yul Brynner’s eyes in Westworld, the 1973

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.240
movie where he plays a robot cowboy.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.240
This is not a spoiler.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.470
I mean, he’s a robot on the poster, and
if you’ve seen the HBO series,

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.770
you know that everything is a robot.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:27.240
It’s this pixelated robot’s-eye-view that
gave birth to CGI: computer generated imagery.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.520
But the technique and idea did not come from
Hollywood.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.220
It came from a bit further away.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:40.390
This is a picture of Mars.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.800
The CGI was groundbreaking, so now we’re
gonna see if we can get our computer graphics

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.340
team to replicate it.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.470
Oh yeah.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:49.600
I’m done.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.430
It takes, like, two clicks.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:58.250
But in the early 1970s, computers this powerful
were non-existent, and digital images were

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.550
rare.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:05.630
These title swirls in 1958’s Vertigo were
sort of computer generated imagery — the

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.710
designer repurposed an M5 anti-aircraft gun’s
mechanical computer — to help draw these

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.630
intricate patterns.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.579
Other experiments were digital, but they were
basically art films.

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:28.030
After all, non-CGI effects could generate
stunning results.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:38.399
Still, Westworld creator Michael Crichton
wanted to create a robot’s point of view.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:40.749
Hollywood hadn’t done it yet.

00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:42.299
But NASA had.

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:43.380
“Liftoff.”

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.689
When NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory launched
Mariner 4, it was the first flyby of Mars,

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:54.549
and it transmitted back the first up-close
photographs of the planet.

00:01:54.549 --> 00:02:00.009
The method it used to get those images to
Earth was important for two reasons: it shaped

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:04.329
what a “digital picture” was should look
like — and it gave technical guidance for

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:06.039
the Westworld CGI.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:08.670
The pictures were sent back as numbers.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:15.480
“240,000 bits of binary code, representing
the shading of 40,000 dots that will finally

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.099
make up the first picture.”

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:23.010
“A complex system of computers is required
to convert these numbers into pictures.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:28.410
Some workers decide to handmake their own
picture of Mars by shading the numbers.”

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:32.930
Yes, our first up close picture of Mars — it
was a paint by numbers.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:39.040
Ultimately, the computer generated images
ended up looking like these.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:45.890
In the 1973 cover story of American Cinematographer,
Westworld designer John Whitney Jr. wrote

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.689
that “the scanning digitizing methods employed
by Jet Propulsion Laboratory on their Mariner

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:54.420
Mars flybys could be used here.”

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.980
But using the rare computer at the lab would
have cost 100 grand.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.900
They found a private company to basically
copy the Jet Propulsion Lab’s methods to

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.629
“scan production footage, frame by frame,
and convert it to numerical information,”

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:12.060
which would then be played back on a special
machine and re-recorded.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:18.560
To make the perfect robot vision, the designers
used data that created an image with 3,600

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.239
squares.

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:24.019
And they had to get creative to make sure
the CGI action came through clearly.

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:28.629
Actors wore clothing to contrast with the
background - here’s one wearing white clothing

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:29.629
and makeup.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:31.859
They also increased the contrast in post-production.

00:03:31.859 --> 00:03:37.400
And though it took a full minute to scan each
frame — or about eight hours for a ten-second

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.590
sequence — the CGI worked.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.480
Hollywood couldn’t have — sorry, can you
just...

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.459
Hollywood couldn’t have come up with this
idea alone.

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:52.129
It took the R&amp;D from the $550 million Mariner
program to inspire something as fanciful as

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:55.749
CGI, especially when practical effects could
have gotten the job done.

00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:57.609
But it was the future.

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:01.989
Whitney finished that article for American
Cinematographer by saying,

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:12.010
“My work on Westworld suggested many more
possibilities than we were able to explore,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:18.600
and there are certainly many others yet to
be imagined.”

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.080
OK, so this edition of Almanac’s all about
big changes to the movies that came from outside

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.420
of Hollywood, so if you know of any sciencey
type innovations like what you just saw that

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.680
changed movies, let me know in the comments.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.170
There’s a lot out there.

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:45.510
However, I cannot end this video without letting
you know about Futureworld.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:51.030
It is the sort of forgotten sequel to Westworld,
but the CGI is actually still influential,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.720
and that’s because it’s likely the source
of the first 3D CGI face.

